INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Pandora Papers reporting on King Abdullah II property network", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2021/oct/03/king-of-jordan-hidden-property-empire-worth-more-than-100m-pandora-papers-reveal", "text": "Benchmark sources report that the Pandora Papers showed King Abdullah II as beneficial owner of an offshore property network. The Guardian says a February 2017 Alcogal memorandum identified a Jordanian client called Abdullah Al Hussein, born 30 January 1962 and with Raghadan Palace address, as beneficial owner of 16 companies holding assets in the US, UK and Jersey. Named companies included Nabisco Holdings SA, Setara Limited and Timara Limited; the latter three Malibu properties were acquired through them. The Guardian reports three Malibu clifftop properties, including a 26-room mansion bought in August 2014 for $33.5m and two neighboring properties; three Washington DC condominiums bought in the prior two years for $13.8m total; and seven luxury UK properties bought 2003-2011, including three in Belgravia, with estimated current value about £28m. The overall portfolio was reported as well over $100m. It says companies were incorporated in the British Virgin Islands, while the network held assets in the US, UK and Jersey. Alcogal staff allegedly avoided identifying the client as a politically exposed person and used the phrase 'you know who'; Alcogal denied deliberately failing to identify a PEP and said identity details were recorded in BVI's secure beneficial-ownership system in November 2017. The Guardian says there was no evidence of wrongdoing. The palace and DLA Piper lawyers said properties were personally funded, used for official/private visits, and acquired through offshore companies for privacy/security; they denied use of public monies or aid. Other reporting (SRC-001) says 14 houses/flats in US and UK, 2003-2017, totaling over €91m/$106m, via 36 front companies registered in tax havens, and that the royal court said purchases were not funded by state treasury, grants or loans. SRC-003/SRC-004 report three Malibu beachfront mansions bought through three offshore companies for $68m, three Washington apartments, an Ascot house and multimillion-dollar central London apartments; ICIJ investigation involved 11.9m leaked documents from 14 financial services. Al Jazeera says jurisdictions represented in the broader leak included BVI, Panama, Belize, Cyprus, UAE, Singapore and Switzerland, but this is not evidence that Abdullah's entities were in all those jurisdictions."}
